course: dictionary
lesson: 10619
title: Adverbs: how something is done
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An adverb tells you how, when or where something happens. Many adverbs end in -ly, and you can make one by adding -ly to an adjective: fair becomes fairly. Unfairly is the adverb form of unfair, so it tells you how something is done.
explain: Unfairly means in a way that is not fair, not right or not equal for everyone. Look at the verb it goes with: «Arguing is fine; arguing unfairly is the problem.» The word unfairly tells you how the arguing is done, and that is the problem.
explain: Read the words around it for clues: «If you think a rule was applied unfairly, you can say so calmly later.» Applied unfairly means the rule was not used the same way for everyone. Try your meaning in the sentence: "applied in a way that is not equal for everyone" still makes sense, so you have it.
explain: Unfairly is also used in maths when things are shared. If one child gets most of the sweets and the others get almost none, the sweets were shared unfairly, because the groups are not equal.
fact: An adverb describes how, when or where an action happens.
fact: «Unfairly» means in a way that is not fair, not right or not equal for everyone.
fact: Unfair is an adjective. You make the adverb by adding -ly: unfair becomes unfairly.
fact: Useful phrases: «treated unfairly», «blamed unfairly», «shared unfairly».
fact: In maths sharing problems, things shared unfairly are not split into equal groups.
exercise: Ruby was «blamed unfairly» for breaking the vase. What does unfairly mean here? | *In a way that is not fair, because she did not break it | Loudly and quickly, in a rush | Happily, with a smile | Very carefully, with great attention
exercise: Four children shared 8 biscuits. One child got 5 and the others got 1 each. How were the biscuits shared? | Carefully, with a knife | Quickly, in the playground | Fairly, because everyone had some | *Unfairly, because the groups are not equal
exercise: Find the adverb in «Arguing is fine; arguing unfairly is the problem.» | problem | arguing | *unfairly | fine
test: Unfairly is the adverb made from which adjective? | fairly | fairy | *unfair | unfairness
test: «A rule was applied unfairly.» What does this mean? | Used by the teachers every single day | Changed to be stricter for the whole school | Written down on paper for everyone to read | *Not used in the same way for everyone
exam: Which sentence uses unfairly to tell how something was done? | Lia is an unfair girl who likes books | *Lia was blamed unfairly for the broken window, though she was at home | Unfairly, the bell rang at lunchtime | The unfairly sun was shining over the field
